    "exact_replacement": "If it were infinite, for each positive integer $n$ the set of ordered $n$-tuples of pairwise distinct orbits would be nonempty. Countable Choice selects one such tuple for every $n$ simultaneously. Flatten these already selected finite tuples into one sequence and retain each orbit only at its first occurrence. Their union is infinite because it contains at least $n$ distinct elements for every $n$, so this gives a sequence of distinct orbits without dependent selection. Countable Choice then selects one representative from each of these prescribed orbits."
